    isbefore: []  # 549d
    duplicate_of: []  # 549d
    not_duplicate_of: []  # 549d
    pr_ref:  # 549d
    description: "INV-linub L3 RESIDUAL FOR GO, the sibling of WI-dizag (rust struct-field) and\nWI-papar (rust chained / try-expression). Found while adjudicating INV-kanuk;\nit is the reason INV-kanuk's row removal is NOT licensed.\n\nMEASURED 2026-08-29 on four fixtures (rig: ~/hypergumbo_lab_notebook/kanuk_go_08292026):\n\n  form                                     dst emitted                match?\n  param decl   func f(conn net.Conn)       go:net:0-0:Read            YES\n  var decl     var conn net.Conn           go:net:0-0:Read            YES\n  in-repo ret  conn := makeConn() net.Conn go:external:0-0:Read       NO\n  stdlib ret   ln, _ := net.Listen(...)    go:external:0-0:Accept     NO\n\nA DECLARED external receiver type reaches the io-boundary module slot and the\ncatalogue row matches. A type INFERRED FROM A RETURN VALUE does not -- the slot\nis filled with `external` and no `net.Conn.Read` / `net.Listener.Accept` row can\never match it.\n\nNOT AN EXTERNAL-BOUNDARY GAP. The positive control settles this: `makeConn()`\nis declared IN THE ANALYSED REPO with return type `net.Conn`, so the INV-dihos\nreturn-type registry has something to register -- and `conn.Read()` STILL emits\n`go:external:0-0:Read`. So this is not \"the stdlib has no declaration to\nregister\"; it is L3, the module slot, exactly WI-dizag's shape in another\nlanguage. Without that control I would have filed the wrong mechanism.\n\nNOT A DUPLICATE OF INV-dihos (satisfied, correctly). That item is about SYMBOL\nRESOLUTION -- `qry.Close()` resolving to the wrong in-repo symbol -- and its\nPhase 1 var-assignment chaining works. This is about the io_boundary MODULE\nSLOT, a different consumer of the same inferred type. An item can be satisfied\nfor resolution and still leak here, which is precisely the L1/L2/L3 chain\nINV-linub was filed to hold.\n\nWHY IT MATTERS MORE THAN A MISSING ROW. On an idiomatic Go accept loop --\n`ln, _ := net.Listen(...)`, `conn, _ := ln.Accept()`, `conn.Read(buf)` -- the\nwhole receive path is return-value-inferred, so NONE of it classifies.\nMeasured on that fixture: `net_recv` reports FOUR chains and every one of them\nis a setup call that receives nothing (syscall.Socket / Bind / Listen,\nnet.Listen). Go's true network-input surface on the canonical server shape is\nEMPTY, and the only thing making it look non-empty is four false sources.\n`external_potential_edges` is 0, so these calls are not even disclosed as\nunresolved -- they are simply absent.\n\nCONSEQUENCE FOR INV-kanuk, WHICH IS WHY THIS BLOCKS IT. ADR-0049 ruling 3\nlicenses a removal only while the crossing stays represented. INV-kanuk's filed\nlicence -- \"net.Conn.Read and syscall.Recvfrom carry the actual receive\" -- is\ntrue of the CATALOGUE and false of the RUN. Deleting the four firing rows takes\ngo's network ingress to zero rather than relocating it, which is the WI-lunav\nfailure at four rows instead of one.\n\nSUGGESTED SHAPE OF THE FIX: carry the inferred type through to the module slot\nfor return-value-inferred receivers, the same repair WI-dizag needs for rust\nstruct fields. Check whether one shared mechanism serves both before writing\neither -- three L3 residuals in three languages (this, WI-dizag, WI-papar) plus\nINV-mumov for python attribute chains is a family, not four bugs.\n\nREPRO\n  mkdir -p /tmp/goprobe && cd /tmp/goprobe && cat > go.mod <<'M'\n  module probe\n  go 1.21\n  M\n  cat > p.go <<'G'\n  package main\n  import \"net\"\n  func makeConn() net.Conn { var c net.Conn; return c }\n  func inRepoChain()  { conn := makeConn(); buf := make([]byte, 16); _, _ = conn.Read(buf) }\n  func declared(conn net.Conn) { buf := make([]byte, 16); _, _ = conn.Read(buf) }\n  G\n  hypergumbo survey /tmp/goprobe --out /tmp/goprobe/s.json\n  # declared    -> go:net:0-0:Read       (matches net.Conn.Read)\n  # inRepoChain -> go:external:0-0:Read  (matches nothing)"  # 549d
    fields: {}  # 549d
